    kind: work_item  # 65a5
    title: "tracker tags rename verb cannot rename FROM a legacy-format (regex-violating) tag"  # 65a5
    status: todo_hard  # 65a5
    priority: 3  # 65a5
    parent:  # 65a5
    tags: []  # 65a5
    isbefore: []  # 65a5
    duplicate_of: []  # 65a5
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 65a5
    pr_ref:  # 65a5
    description: "## Origin\n\nSurfaced 2026-05-07 during a tag-naming hygiene cleanup: the `awaits_bakeoff_validation` queue had 21 items tagged with hyphenated names `for-deep-bakeoff` and `for-uat-bakeoff`. The hyphenated tags predated the `_TAG_NAME_RE = ^[a-z_][a-z0-9_]*$` validator wiring on the `tracker add --tag` / `tracker update --add-tag` write paths; they were grandfathered in via the catalog backfill. Renaming them to snake_case via the editorial verb `tracker tags rename` is the policy-compliant migration path — but the verb itself is structurally broken when the old name violates the validator.\n\n## Symptom\n\n```bash\n$ tracker tags rename for-deep-bakeoff for_deep_bakeoff\nerror: tag name 'for-deep-bakeoff' does not match ^[a-z_][a-z0-9_]*$\n```\n\nThe error message comes from the catalog save step, not the rename's own `--new` validator (cli.py:1294 only validates `new`).\n\n## Root cause\n\nIn `_cmd_tags_rename` at `packages/hypergumbo-tracker/src/hypergumbo_tracker/cli.py:1283-1347`:\n\n1. The per-item rename loop (lines 1306-1318) iterates items, calling `ts.update(item.id, add_fields={...}, remove_fields={\"tags\": [old]})`. This **writes per-item ops successfully** — `--remove-tag` accepts a literal string without validating it against the regex.\n\n2. After the per-item loop, the verb opens the catalog (line 1327: `tag_catalog.load_catalog(...)`) and bumps `last_modified` on **both** endpoints (lines 1329-1338). The docstring at lines 1322-1324 cites the design intent: \"Always record the old endpoint in the catalog (even if it wasn't catalogued before) so the audit trail captures both halves of the rename.\"\n\n3. The catalog save call at line 1339 invokes `save_catalog()`, which at `tag_catalog.py:289-293` validates **every** entry's name against `_TAG_NAME_RE` before serializing:\n   ```python\n   for name, entry in catalog.items():\n       if not _TAG_NAME_RE.match(name):\n           raise ValueError(\n               f\"tag name {name!r} does not match {_TAG_NAME_RE.pattern}\",\n           )\n   ```\n\n4. Old endpoint fails the regex → `save_catalog()` raises → the rename verb prints the error and exits non-zero. Per-item ops are **already durable** (committed to .ops files); only the catalog audit-trail entry never lands.\n\nNet effect: the rename verb that's supposed to be the canonical migration path for legacy-format tags **cannot operate on legacy-format tags**. Users who hit this either (a) abandon the rename, leaving tags inconsistent forever, or (b) fall back to a per-item `tracker update --remove-tag OLD --add-tag NEW` loop (which works but skips the catalog audit trail, the same trail the rename's design rationale wanted to preserve).\n\n## Why this matters\n\nThe validator-on-write tightening was a forward-only policy: existing legacy-format tags stayed in items, and the catalog backfill walked the op log skipping the invalid names. The editorial verbs (`tags rename` / `describe` / `deprecate`) exist precisely so users can fix legacy state without dropping to direct YAML edits. When the editorial verb that's supposed to fix this state itself enforces the same forward-only policy on its inputs, the migration path closes — there is no within-tracker way to recover from the historical inconsistency.\n\nEmpirically: 21 items needed migration today; the rename verb couldn't do it; the workaround was a 21-iteration loop of per-item updates. Each per-item update emitted the same catalog-save error message at the end of its run, but the per-item ops landed correctly. End state was right, audit trail wasn't, error noise was 21x what should have been a single clean rename.\n\n## Fix scope (two options)\n\n**(a) Skip the audit-trail bump for legacy-format old endpoints.** In `_cmd_tags_rename`, before line 1329, check `if not _TAG_NAME_RE.match(old): catalog.pop(old, None)` instead of upserting `old_entry`. The audit trail loses one record (the bump on the legacy-format old endpoint) but the rename completes cleanly and the catalog converges to a valid state.\n\n**(b) Migrate the catalog forward simultaneously.** Treat the rename as a structural fix: replace any legacy-format `old` entries in the catalog with the snake_case `new` entry rather than retaining both. Same end state as (a) but with explicit re-keying rather than implicit drop. Cleaner from a \"what does the catalog represent\" standpoint.\n\nOption (a) is smaller; option (b) is more semantically correct. Either resolves the structural break.\n\nSubordinate concern: the error message itself is misleading. `error: tag name 'for-deep-bakeoff' does not match ^[a-z_][a-z0-9_]*$` doesn't tell the user this is the **catalog audit step** failing, not the rename input validation. A better message would be `error: legacy-format old tag {old!r} cannot be retained in catalog after rename` (or, after the fix, suppressed entirely).\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- New unit test: `tracker tags rename` from a legacy-format tag to a snake_case tag completes cleanly (exit 0, no error message), per-item ops land, catalog converges to a valid state with the snake_case entry only.\n- Regression test: `tracker tags rename` between two snake_case tags continues to record both endpoints in the catalog audit trail.\n- 100% coverage on changed sources.\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- The 21-item migration that surfaced this bug: WI-davan, WI-likab, WI-lidum, WI-dohaf, WI-vavur, WI-ribav, WI-habut, WI-vumum, WI-zarov, WI-mihiz, WI-nunal, WI-kagik, WI-nibis, WI-gajob, WI-dubab, WI-kunag (deep-bakeoff queue), WI-sulaz, WI-halat, WI-sokaz, WI-nutav, WI-nosug, WI-zozuz (uat-bakeoff queue). All migrated successfully via the per-item workaround.\n- Validator definition: `packages/hypergumbo-tracker/src/hypergumbo_tracker/tag_catalog.py:121`.\n- Save-validation enforcement: `tag_catalog.py:289-293`.\n- Rename verb implementation: `cli.py:1283-1347`.\n- BVT discipline (the queue tags being migrated): `.agent/agent_playbooks_protocols_sops_skills/bakeoff-validation-tagging-discipline.md`.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Whether the `_TAG_NAME_RE` regex itself should allow hyphens — that's a governance question (AGENTS.md tag-naming convention), not a tracker bug. The validator was tightened deliberately; this item only fixes the rename verb's interaction with already-existing legacy data.\n- The error-message clarity improvement is a nice-to-have, not a hard requirement; defer if it complicates the fix."  # 65a5
    fields: {}  # 65a5
